# File: graf-tiles.prf

#
# This file defines tile mappings for most game entities for when graphics mode
# is enabled and tiles graphics with the MicroChasm tileset are used.
# Tile mappings for flavored objects/items are defined separately in
# `flvr-tiles.prf`, which is included at the bottom of this file.
#
# Tile mappings assign game entities such as monsters to tiles in the MicroChasm
# tileset image. For legacy reasons, these tile mappings are expressed as
# attr/char mappings, which are interpreted as an index into the tileset image
# (attr = y = row, char = x = column).
#
# The easiest way to understand and work with these tile mappings is
# using `lib/xtra/graf/TilePicker.html` to visually explore the tileset.
#
#
# === Understanding graf-tiles.prf ===
#
# S: index : attr/char
# F: feature index : attr/char
# K: object index : attr/char
# R: monster/player index : attr/char
# R: monster/player index : rage : attr/char
#
# 'S' is for specials (visual effects). The index identifies the specific
#     effect type (e.g., digits 0-9, arrows, breath effects, spell impacts).
#     The attr/char value is a hex pair (e.g., 0x8C/0x80) representing the
#     tile position in the tileset image (attr = row, char = column).
#
# 'F' is for features (dungeon terrain). The feature index corresponds to
#     the feature type defined in the game code (e.g., floor, walls, doors,
#     traps, stairs, forges). The attr/char value specifies the tile position.
#
# 'K' is for objects (items/equipment). The object index corresponds to the
#     serial number ('N:' line) in `lib/edit/object.txt`. The attr/char value
#     specifies the tile position.
#
# 'R' is for (races of) player and monsters. For player races (indices 0-3),
#     the index corresponds to Noldor (0), Sindar (1), Naugrim (2), and
#     Edain (3). For monsters (indices 11+), the index corresponds to the
#     serial number ('N:' line) in `lib/edit/monster.txt`. The attr/char value
#     specifies the tile position.
#     You can also add optional 'R:<id>:rage:<attr>/<char>' lines. When the
#     player is raging, this alternative tile is displayed instead of the
#     monster's normal tile (the rage shader still tints whichever tile is
#     chosen). Omitting a line for a given monster means it keeps its regular
#     tile during rage. Such 'R:<id>:rage:...' lines must always have a
#     corresponding regular 'R:<id>:' line.
#

###############################################################################
# Specials
###############################################################################

## # Digits - 0 - 9
S:0x00:0x8C/0x80
S:0x01:0x8C/0x81
S:0x02:0x8C/0x82
S:0x03:0x8C/0x83
